<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Hira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hira was an ancient city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, south of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, capital of the Arab Lakhmid kings from the late 3rd century until 602. A centre of Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and early Arabic poetry, it served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> as a frontier base facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the desert tribes. It surrendered to the Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> in 633 and declined after Kufa was founded nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hira.jpg" alt="Al-Hira" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamal-ud-din_Bihzad_-_Construction_of_the_fort_of_Kharnaq.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Al-Hira was an ancient city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, in what is now south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, on the western bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> a few miles south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>. It lay on the northwestern edge of the Samarra Desert between the river and a seasonal lake known as the Najaf Sea. Its open connection to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> meant that Arab tribes could reach it freely, and canals from the Euphrates made the region one of the most fertile in Iraq. The name probably derives from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> word meaning enclosure, referring to the first permanent encampment of Lakhmid leadership.</p><p>The city was sometimes called the White, because of the quantity of its buildings. Little is known of its society, since most histories come from early Abbasid times and have been substantially reworked into a narrative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the Lakhmids, Al-Hira was capital of the major Arab client kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. Its kings ruled Arab populations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> deep into Eastern and Central Arabia through a divide-and-rule policy, appointing tribesmen as kings, military leaders and tax collectors. Al-Nu'man I ibn Imru al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>, who reigned from 403 to 431, abdicated to live as a monk, and is credited with building the palaces of Al-Khawarnaq and Al-Sadir. The court reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-numan-iii-ibn-al-mundhir">Al-Nu'man III ibn al-Mundhir</a>, from 580 to 602, attracting poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nabigha-al-dhubyani">Al-Nabigha al-Dhubyani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ibn-thabit">Hassan ibn Thabit</a>. After Al-Nu'man III converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and defied Sasanian orders he was removed, and the kingdom was absorbed into the Sasanian Empire in 602.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> came to the city in the fourth century, and by 410 it had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. It became a base for missions to the Sasanian world and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> of the desert; the Chronicle of Seert records the work of the monastic founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> of Kashkar there. Al-Hira was a multilingual crossroads, and its Hiri script, derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> alphabet, is considered a precursor of the Arabic script.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Al-Hira surrendered peacefully in 633, after a brief siege led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>'s army, one of the first major cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to fall to Muslim control. It lost political significance to the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> but remained inhabited until the tenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-abbas-al-saffah">Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> stayed there. By the 13th century it had ceased to function as a city. Some inhabitants migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, where a quarter bore the name of Al-Hira.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Hira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under al-Harith ibn Amr in the early 6th century, Kinda reached its peak and even held <b>al-Hira</b> for a time at the expense of the Lakhmid Kingdom, reportedly installed there by the Sasanian king Kavad I.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kinda">Kingdom of Kinda</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lakhmids, or Nasrids, established themselves at <b>al-Hira</b> near the Euphrates in the late 3rd century as allies of the Sasanian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/lakhmid-kingdom">Lakhmid Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Arabic tradition says the Tanukh were a coalition of tribes that migrated from eastern Arabia into Iraq, where some settled around <b>al-Hira</b>, while others moved into Syria in the later 3rd century.</blockquote><a href="/state/tanukhids">Tanukhids</a></li></ul></section>
